+# Invoicing
+
+Turns an order into an invoice. The calculation lives in `src/invoice.ts` (invoice totals),
+`src/pricing.ts` (individual item lines) and `src/money.ts` (rounding and formatting).
+
+Run the tests with:
+
+```sh
+node --experimental-strip-types --test "tests/**/*.test.ts"
+```
+
+## How invoice totals are calculated
+
+This section explains, without code, how every number on an invoice is produced.
+
+### Rounding
+
+Every amount is rounded to the smallest unit of the invoice currency:
+
+| Currency | Rounded to        |
+| -------- | ----------------- |
+| USD      | the cent (0.01)   |
+| EUR      | the cent (0.01)   |
+| JPY      | the whole yen (1) |
+
+Rounding is to the nearest unit, and an amount exactly halfway is rounded **up**
+(for example $1.4985 → $1.50, $0.705 → $0.71, ¥12.5 → ¥13). For yen this follows the
+Japanese entity's accounting policy (Finance ticket FIN-88).
+
+> Note: the system stores amounts as ordinary decimal-like numbers, not as whole cents. In rare
+> cases an amount that looks exactly halfway (such as 1.005) is held internally as a hair below
+> halfway and rounds down instead of up.
+
+### Inputs taken from the order
+
+- **Items** — each with a quantity and a unit price.
+- **Order discount rate** — e.g. 10%. Applies to every item unless the item has its own rate.
+- **Item discount rate** (optional) — a promotion on one item. It *replaces* the order discount
+  rate for that item only; it is not added on top. An item rate of 0% means "no discount on
+  this item", even if the order has one.
+- **Tax rate** — e.g. 8.25%. Applies to every item, after discount.
+- **Shipping fee** (optional) — a flat amount.
+
+### Kinds of line on an invoice
+
+An invoice can contain three kinds of line, always in this order:
+
+1. **Item lines** — one per item on the order, in the order they were entered. These are always
+   present.
+2. **Shipping line** (code `SHIP`, "Shipping") — present only if the order has a shipping fee
+   greater than zero. Shipping is charged exactly as given: it is **never discounted and never
+   taxed**. A missing, zero or negative shipping fee produces no shipping line and adds nothing.
+3. **Rounding adjustment line** (code `ADJ`, "Rounding adjustment") — present only when needed,
+   see [Why a rounding adjustment line exists](#why-a-rounding-adjustment-line-exists). It can be
+   positive or negative and is usually a few cents.
+
+Shipping and adjustment lines show quantity 1, no discount and no tax; their gross and total are
+both the shipping fee / adjustment amount.
+
+### How each item line is calculated
+
+For each item, in this order, rounding at every step:
+
+1. **Gross** = quantity × unit price, rounded.
+2. **Discount** = gross × the item's discount rate (its own rate if it has one, otherwise the
+   order rate), rounded.
+3. **Tax** = (gross − discount) × tax rate, rounded.
+4. **Total** = gross − discount + tax.
+
+Because gross, discount and tax are each already rounded, the line total needs no further rounding.
+
+### How the invoice totals are calculated
+
+The invoice totals are **not** made by adding up the lines. They are calculated once, on the whole
+order, and rounded once:
+
+1. **Subtotal** = the sum of quantity × unit price over all items, rounded. Shipping is not included.
+2. **Discount total** =
+   - (the sum of quantity × unit price over the items that use the order rate, rounded) × the
+     order discount rate,
+   - plus, for each item with its own rate, quantity × unit price × that item's rate
+     (not rounded individually),
+   - with the combined figure rounded once.
+3. **Tax total** = (subtotal − discount total) × tax rate, rounded.
+4. **Shipping total** = the shipping fee, rounded (0 if there is none, or if it is zero or negative).
+5. **Total** = subtotal − discount total + tax total + shipping total, rounded.
+
+The invoice total is always this figure.
+
+### How the lines relate to the invoice total
+
+Item lines are rounded one at a time, while the invoice totals are rounded once on the whole order.
+The small rounding differences on each line add up, so the sum of the line totals can differ from
+the invoice total by a few cents (more lines means potentially more difference).
+
+#### Why a rounding adjustment line exists
+
+So that the lines on the invoice always add up exactly to the invoice total, the system compares
+the two and, if they differ, adds a **rounding adjustment** line for the full difference:
+
+> adjustment = invoice total − (sum of item line totals + shipping line total)
+
+If there is no difference, no adjustment line is added. With it, **the line totals always add up
+exactly to the invoice total**.
+
+Only the line *totals* are reconciled. The discount and tax columns of the lines are not, so
+adding up the lines' discount (or tax) column may give a figure a few cents off the invoice's
+discount total (or tax total). The invoice-level figures are the authoritative ones.
+
+### Worked example
+
+Order ORD-3003, in US dollars: order discount 15%, tax 8.25%, shipping $7.50, no item-level
+discounts.
+
+| Item   | Description | Qty | Unit price |
+| ------ | ----------- | --: | ---------: |
+| MUG-01 | Mug         |   1 |      $9.99 |
+| HOOD-3 | Hoodie      |   1 |     $29.99 |
+| BOOT-4 | Boots       |   2 |     $49.99 |
+
+**Step 1 — item lines** (each value rounded to the cent as it is calculated):
+
+| Item   | Gross                  | Discount (15%)             | Tax (8.25% of gross − discount) | Line total |
+| ------ | ---------------------- | -------------------------- | ------------------------------- | ---------: |
+| MUG-01 | 1 × 9.99 = **9.99**    | 1.4985 → **1.50**          | 8.49 × 0.0825 = 0.7004 → **0.70**  |   **9.19** |
+| HOOD-3 | 1 × 29.99 = **29.99**  | 4.4985 → **4.50**          | 25.49 × 0.0825 = 2.1029 → **2.10** |  **27.59** |
+| BOOT-4 | 2 × 49.99 = **99.98**  | 14.997 → **15.00**         | 84.98 × 0.0825 = 7.0109 → **7.01** |  **91.99** |
+| Sum    | 139.96                 | 21.00                      | 9.81                            |     128.77 |
+
+**Step 2 — invoice totals** (calculated on the whole order, rounded once):
+
+| Figure         | Calculation                                   | Result      |
+| -------------- | --------------------------------------------- | ----------: |
+| Subtotal       | 9.99 + 29.99 + 99.98                          |  **139.96** |
+| Discount total | 139.96 × 15% = 20.994 → rounded               |   **20.99** |
+| Tax total      | (139.96 − 20.99) × 8.25% = 118.97 × 0.0825 = 9.8150 → rounded | **9.82** |
+| Shipping total | flat fee, no discount, no tax                 |    **7.50** |
+| Total          | 139.96 − 20.99 + 9.82 + 7.50                  |  **136.29** |
+
+Note how the item lines' discount column adds up to 21.00 and tax column to 9.81, while the invoice
+shows 20.99 and 9.82: that is the one-at-a-time vs. once-overall rounding difference.
+
+**Step 3 — reconcile the lines with the total:**
+
+- Item lines: 9.19 + 27.59 + 91.99 = 128.77
+- Plus shipping line: 128.77 + 7.50 = 136.27
+- Invoice total: 136.29
+- Difference: 136.29 − 136.27 = **+0.02** → a rounding adjustment line of $0.02 is added.
+
+**The resulting invoice:**
+
+| Line   | Description         | Qty | Gross  | Discount | Tax  |  Total |
+| ------ | ------------------- | --: | -----: | -------: | ---: | -----: |
+| MUG-01 | Mug                 |   1 |   9.99 |     1.50 | 0.70 |   9.19 |
+| HOOD-3 | Hoodie              |   1 |  29.99 |     4.50 | 2.10 |  27.59 |
+| BOOT-4 | Boots               |   2 |  99.98 |    15.00 | 7.01 |  91.99 |
+| SHIP   | Shipping            |   1 |   7.50 |     0.00 | 0.00 |   7.50 |
+| ADJ    | Rounding adjustment |   1 |   0.02 |     0.00 | 0.00 |   0.02 |
+|        | **Total**           |     |        |          |      | **136.29** |
+
+Subtotal 139.96 · Discount total 20.99 · Tax total 9.82 · Shipping 7.50 · **Total 136.29**
